\section*{The universal quantum-depletion constant for fixed bounded repulsions}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The same leading depletion coefficient is claimed for every fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potential of positive scattering length, uniformly over ground-state density matrices.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Local density tails and ground-state path bounds control occupations at the relevant scattering scale. Spatial comparison transports these local estimates to the constant orbital, identifying every thermodynamic accumulation value before density tends to zero.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The same leading depletion coefficient is claimed for every fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potential of positive scattering length, uniformly over ground-state density matrices. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 267, the selected statement is a density-uniform hard-sphere ground-state condensate lower bound for pure and mixed ground states. Positive-temperature condensation and quantum-depletion asymptotics are beyond it. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

